  "evidence": "TEXT_E: eliminate the bulk of the structural deficit in a Parliament with an emergency Budget and immediate cuts; annual cap on non‑EU economic migrants and tighter student visas; tougher knife sentences, elected police commissioners, Border Police Force; protect real-terms NHS funding with patient choice; recall MPs, cut number of MPs, radical transparency. TEXT_F: avoid premature cuts and halve the deficit by 2014 while protecting frontline services; control immigration via points‑based system and earned citizenship; protect NHS budgets with guarantees; AV referendum, lobbyists’ register, recall; jobs/training guarantees and bank bonus tax.",
  "reasoning": "In 2010 voters prioritised swift action on the deficit, tighter immigration control, crime and policing, NHS protection, and cleaning up politics. TEXT_E addresses these with clearer, firmer commitments (rapid deficit reduction, an immigration cap, tougher crime/border measures, and political reform), whereas TEXT_F emphasises slower consolidation and a softer approach on immigration, making TEXT_E a closer match to those priorities.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
